description:
In crate-jmap-tasks-types/src/task_list.rs:36-43, `TaskListRole::to_wire_str` manually maps variants to wire strings:

    pub fn to_wire_str(&self) -> &str {
        match self {
            Self::Inbox => "inbox",
            Self::Trash => "trash",
            Self::Other(s) => s.as_str(),
        }
    }

This duplicates the string mapping declared in the `impl_string_enum!` macro invocation directly above (lines 29-32). A future contributor adding a new variant (say `Archive`) must remember to update two places: the macro invocation AND the to_wire_str manual match. The compiler does not enforce this — the macro would happily compile with a new variant the manual match doesn't cover (because of the `Self::Other(_)` arm acting as a catch-all... wait, no — `Other` is a specific variant, so a new `Archive` variant would be a compile-time error in the manual match, which is fortunate).

The redundancy is mild because the compile error guards against forgetting. But it is still redundancy: the macro already implements Serialize, which can be invoked via `serde_json::to_value(self)` (or to_string) to get the wire string. A one-liner wrapper that delegates to the Serialize impl would be sufficient.

The sister type `TaskProgress` (task.rs:19) does NOT have an analogous `to_wire_str`. Either both should have one, or neither should — consistency drives the choice.

Fix: drop `to_wire_str` and let callers do `serde_json::to_string(&role).unwrap_or_default()`. If a non-allocating wire-str view is needed in a hot path, add the helper to the `impl_string_enum!` macro itself so it covers every consumer uniformly. Labeled `opinion` because some teams prefer the explicit match for searchability.
